/**
 * @page core_model Core model: datasets, trees, and primitives
 *
 * The core model is the vocabulary shared by search, evaluation, and execution.
 * `Tree` owns postfix nodes. `Dataset` owns or views column-major values.
 * `Problem` binds a dataset, train/test/validation ranges, target/input
 * variables, a primitive set, and linear-scaling policy.
 *
 * @section core-model-flow Representation relationships
 *
 * This representation view shows ownership and data dependencies. It does not
 * describe an evaluation workflow; see @ref architecture for the system map.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph core_model {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *   graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.55, ranksep=0.75];
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.12,0.08"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#4a5568", fontcolor="#4a5568", arrowsize=0.7];
 *   dataset [label="Dataset"];
 *   pset [label="PrimitiveSet"];
 *   problem [label="Problem"];
 *   creator [label="Creators / variation"];
 *   individual [label="Individual\nTree genotype + fitness"];
 *   evaluator [label="Evaluator"];
 *   interpreter [label="Interpreter"];
 *   problem -> dataset [label="owns or borrows; selects ranges"];
 *   problem -> pset [label="owns generation vocabulary"];
 *   creator -> pset [label="uses"];
 *   creator -> individual [label="constructs"];
 *   evaluator -> problem [label="uses"];
 *   evaluator -> individual [label="scores"];
 *   evaluator -> interpreter [label="uses"];
 *   interpreter -> dataset [label="reads"];
 *   interpreter -> individual [label="executes"];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @section core-model-tree Tree and Node
 *
 * `Tree` is an owning `Vector<Node>` in postfix order. A function node consumes
 * exactly its arity's contiguous, adjacent completed child subtrees; the final
 * node is the sole root. `Tree::Validate()` checks this structural contract and
 * derived metadata (`Length`, `Depth`, `Parent`, and `Level`) without entering a
 * hot path. Search and evaluation assume valid trees.
 *
 * `NodeType` has four categories: Constant, Variable, Ref, and Function.
 * Function nodes represent both built-ins and registered operations; their hash
 * identifies the operation and their caller-supplied arity is stored on the
 * node. `Ref` is structural sharing and must target an earlier node. Forward
 * evaluation copies the referenced primal; reverse differentiation accumulates
 * its adjoint into that target.
 *
 * Coefficients are node values with `Optimize == true`, ordered by node order.
 * `Tree::GetCoefficients()` and `SetCoefficients()` use that order. Applying a
 * coefficient vector therefore requires the same tree topology that produced
 * it. `Tree::Hash()` produces structural or coefficient-aware identity according
 * to `HashMode`; choose the latter for memoization whose result depends on
 * fitted coefficients.
 *
 * `UpdateNodes()`, `Reduce()`, `Simplify()`, and tree surgery must re-establish
 * metadata before passing a tree into evaluators or algorithms. `SetEnabled()`
 * applies to an entire subtree, not only the root node.
 *
 * In this documentation, a candidate is an @c Individual. Its genotype is a
 * @c Tree; its fitness is assigned by evaluation. “Model” refers to the tree
 * together with the problem context that gives its values meaning.
 *
 * @section core-model-data Dataset and Problem
 *
 * An owning `Dataset` stores columns with padded row capacity for SIMD access;
 * `Rows()` is the logical count while `PaddedRows()` includes the zeroed tail.
 * `Dataset::Wrap()` is zero-copy and requires caller-owned, padded, column-major
 * memory to outlive the dataset. A wrapped dataset is not a general mutable
 * dataset. Hashes identify variables across tree nodes, datasets, and dispatch.
 *
 * `Problem` either owns its `Dataset` or borrows one, and always owns its
 * `PrimitiveSet`. It is intentionally non-copyable and non-movable. Changing a
 * target recomputes default inputs; explicit inputs remain explicit. Its
 * train/test/validation ranges select rows rather than create data copies.
 *
 * Linear scaling is formulation state in `Problem`, not a display option.
 * `LinearScalingOmitsNonFinite` must agree with the evaluator's non-finite
 * policy or fitting and scoring may use different effective samples.
 *
 * @section core-model-primitives PrimitiveSet and Grammar
 *
 * `PrimitiveSet` controls stochastic generation: each registered node has a
 * frequency and arity range. Its lazy reachable-length table is shared by tree
 * creators. Configure the set before concurrent creation; mutating it while
 * readers use that cache is unsupported. A custom function must be registered
 * with matching dispatch arity before an interpreter can evaluate a generated
 * tree.
 *
 * `Grammar` is separate: it defines the restricted production language used by
 * exhaustive enumeration. Its configuration reuses `PrimitiveSetConfig` only
 * to choose the supported unary wraps; it does not expose every enabled
 * primitive. In particular, its current grammar does not generate Sub, Div,
 * Pow, or several enabled unary/binary operations. Callers must not assume a
 * PrimitiveSet and Grammar describe the same language.
 *
 * @section core-model-entrypoints API entry points
 *
 * - `operon/core/tree.hpp`: `Tree`, validation, hashing, coefficients, subtree views.
 * - `operon/core/node.hpp`: node representation, built-in hashes, function nodes.
 * - `operon/core/dataset.hpp`: owned/view datasets and variable access.
 * - `operon/core/problem.hpp`: task formulation and row ranges.
 * - `operon/core/pset.hpp`: generation vocabulary and reachability.
 * - `operon/core/grammar.hpp`: enumeration productions and complexity bounds.
 */
